## Decomposition procedure — decide in this order

1. OUTPUT: what the question asks for → answer_columns (apply the answer-columns rules above).
2. QUALIFIERS: every qualifying phrase in the question (among/whose/with attribute conditions) → a filter condition.
3. EXTREME / AGGREGATION: only after qualifiers are fixed, and always over the fully filtered set.
4. JOIN: the minimal path connecting only the tables those decisions require.

## Traceability self-check — before outputting, verify

- every answer_column maps to a phrase in the question that explicitly asks for it;
- every condition maps to a qualifying phrase in the question;
- every table participates in an answer column, a condition, or the join path.

A plan element with no supporting phrase in the question must be dropped.

## Construction discipline for plan values

The plan already carries the aggregation expression / conditions / having split — get
those splits right, the generator copies them:

- Aggregate conditions: a filter constraining the population *inside* an aggregate (a
  share/ratio's numerator or denominator, e.g. "the share of running contracts among
  all loans") belongs to the aggregate expression in answer_columns — never to a
  separate row-level condition, which shrinks the base population instead of
  building the ratio.
- Constraints on an aggregate result (a total that must equal a stated number,
  "...debited 3539 in total") → having, not conditions.
- Person-owned records: when the model links a person table to the fact table only
  through a link table carrying a role column (e.g. disp.type: OWNER / DISPONENT),
  "records for/of a <person>" means the person OWNS them — join through the link
  table and constrain the role column to the owner value the model declares; never
  shortcut the person onto a fact table through a coincidental shared column
  (sharing a district id is not an ownership link).
- Category questions: when the question asks WHICH/WHAT <category> (aim, purpose,
  kind, type, reason, channel), that categorical column is an ANSWER column — the
  grouping dimension — never a filter. With a stated total in the same question
  ("...debiting 3539 in total"), group by the category column and constrain the
  total with having; never invent a category value that the question does not name.
- Extreme questions: express the selection with the extreme field (rank for
  second/nth); keep the ordered measure column OUT of answer_columns unless the
  question asks for that measure itself.
- Dimension filters name the human-readable column (district.A2, client.gender),
  not the id column.

## Common failure modes to guard against

- "list all the X" questions: leaking detail columns (date, amount, status, ...) into answer_columns when the question names none;
- category questions: turning the category column into a filter (often with a made-up value) when the question asks WHICH/WHAT that category is;
- ownership questions: joining the person straight onto a fact table via a shared column instead of through the role link table, and dropping the role (owner) constraint;
- formula questions: leaking formula input columns or entity-name columns into answer_columns;
- computing the extreme before all qualifiers are applied as filters;
- listing columns that exist only for ordering.

## Re-plan discipline

When correction feedback is present, diff against the previous plan and change
only what the feedback demands — keep every element the feedback does not dispute.
